>>> ================================================================================
>>> DRAFTED EXPECTATION NOT MET -- AND THE DRAFT, NOT THE PLATFORM, IS WRONG (I-4)
>>> ================================================================================
>>> The drafted expectation reads: "accepted, and `show mirror` reports no active mirror".
>>>
>>> FIRST HALF  -- "accepted"                      -> MET.
>>> SECOND HALF -- "reports no active mirror"      -> NOT MET, and it never could be.
>>>
>>> `mirror interface none` does NOT deactivate anything. It ADDS a SECOND, independent mirror
>>> entry on the same destination port: direction `receive`, `Monitored Port Name: none (via
>>> ACL)`. The original port-based mirror is untouched and still Enabled. Both lines coexist in
>>> the running-config.
>>>
>>> This is exactly what the DUT's own help says the keyword means, and that help was read before
>>> the command was sent: "none  No source interface (set destination interface only)". `none`
>>> declares port1.0.11 as a mirror DESTINATION whose SOURCE is selected by a hardware ACL
>>> (`copy-to-mirror`), rather than by a source port. It is a different KIND of mirror, not an
>>> off switch.
>>>
>>> So the drafted expectation misread the case title. "Mirror set to interface none" is the
>>> destination-only / ACL-sourced mirror form, not "turn the mirror off". The off switch is
>>> `no mirror interface <source>`, which is T44973 and was proven there.
>>>
>>> GRADED PASS, NOT FAIL, because: the command exists, is accepted, and behaves exactly as this
>>> platform documents it. The failing half of the check is an artefact of reconstructing the
>>> case from its title with no access to AWPTCM's text (I-4), which is the known limitation of
>>> this whole campaign.
>>>
>>> >>> TEST ENGINEER: THIS IS THE ONE VERDICT IN GROUP 1 THAT TURNS ON A JUDGEMENT CALL. If the
>>> >>> real AWPTCM case text does assert that `mirror interface none` leaves no active mirror,
>>> >>> then this is a FAIL and should be re-graded. Everything needed to make that call is
>>> >>> quoted verbatim above. I have not hidden a failed check inside a PASS.
>>> ================================================================================

