STEP 3 -- ISOLATE the `none` mirror, to prove it is independent  ......... PASS
================================================================================
Not in the drafted steps. Added because step 2's output is only interpretable if the two mirror
entries are shown to be independent rather than one entry being mis-rendered. The port-based
mirror was removed, leaving the `none` entry alone:

  x960-56YSQ#configure terminal
  Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
  x960-56YSQ(config)#interface port1.0.11
  % port1.0.11 is currently configured as a mirror-port. Use caution when altering its config
  x960-56YSQ(config-if)#no mirror interface port1.0.10
  x960-56YSQ(config-if)#end
  x960-56YSQ#show mirror
  Mirror Test Port Name: port1.0.11
  Mirror option: Enabled
  Mirror direction: receive
  Monitored Port Name: none (via ACL)

  x960-56YSQ#show running-config interface port1.0.11
  !
  interface port1.0.11
   switchport
   switchport mode access
   mirror interface none
  !

(17:19:22 - 17:19:28 NZDT)

PROVEN: `mirror interface none` is an independent, persistent, destination-only mirror
declaration. Removing the port-based mirror does not remove it, and it survives on its own.

This also answers the drafted step's own aside -- "distinct from T44973's removal in that the
mirror destination config may persist". It does persist, and it persists as a configuration line
in its own right, requiring its own `no` form to clear.

>>> USEFUL BEYOND THIS CASE: this is the mechanism T44990 (ACL: Named Hardware on port
>>> copy-to-mirror IP) needs -- a destination port declared with `mirror interface none`, with
>>> the traffic selected by a hardware ACL `copy-to-mirror` action. T44990 is currently BLOCKED
>>> by I-8 (only one in-bench observation point), not by any missing CLI.

